eeking survivorship support-group resources\",\"explanation\":\"A central relationship in this community is the patient’s stated need for a survivor support group and a request for recommendations, indicating psychosocial or practical support needs as part of survivorship [Data: Entities (6407); Relationships (14588)].\\n\\nThe presence of SUPPORT GROUP as a named entity suggests that resource navigation (finding appropriate survivor communities or services) is part of the overall follow-up ecosystem, complementing clinical surveillance with supportive care [Data: Entities (6407, 60); Relationships (14588)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Annual full skin exams represent a concrete, clinician-led surveillance activity within follow-up care\",\"explanation\":\"FULL SKIN EXAMS are described as ongoing follow-up evaluations performed at least once a year after treatment to monitor for recurrence or new skin cancers, making them a specific operational component of follow-up surveillance [Data: Entities (19)].\\n\\nThe relationships indicate that a DOCTOR performs or oversees these full skin exams, underscoring clinician involvement and accountability within the follow-up plan [Data: Relationships (47); Entities (19)]. Additionally, FOLLOW-UP CARE explicitly mentions routine screening practices such as regular full skin examinations at least annually, aligning the broader follow-up concept with this specific exam modality [Data: Entities (60)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Screening for late effects is explicitly incorporated into survivorship follow-up planning\",\"explanation\":\"SCREENING FOR LATE EFFECTS is defined as monitoring and testing for long-term or late effects caused by treatment, including planning how these effects will be screened during survivorship [Data: Entities (6388)].\\n\\nFOLLOW-UP CARE is directly linked to SCREENING FOR LATE EFFECTS through a relationship stating that survivorship follow-up includes screening for late effects and planning how they will be monitored [Data: Relationships (14629); Entities (60, 6388)]. This indicates that survivorship care planning here includes forward-looking surveillance for treatment consequences, not only recurrence detection.\"},{\"summary\":\"The community captures patient concern about late effects and how they will be monitored\",\"explanation\":\"LATE EFFECTS are defined as long-term effects that appear or persist after treatment and are referenced in survivorship questions about what late effects are caused by treatment and how they will be screened [Data: Entities (6405)].\\n\\nA direct relationship ties FOLLOW-UP CARE to LATE EFFECTS, stating the patient asks what late effects are caused by treatment and how they will be screened during survivorship follow-up [Data: Relationships (14633); Entities (60, 6405)]. This highlights patient information needs and suggests that education and expectation-setting are part of the follow-up process in this community [Data: Entities (60)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Follow-up care also includes structured surveillance pathways for solid nodules\",\"explanation\":\"A relationship links FOLLOW-UP CARE to SOLID NODULES, indicating that follow-up of solid nodules is listed as part of follow-up care planning [Data: Relationships (1570); Entities (60)].\\n\\nThis complements the broader FOLLOW-UP CARE description that, in the context of nodule detection, follow-up involves structured surveillance tailored to nodule characteristics, implying protocolized monitoring over time using imaging and clinical evaluation [Data: Entities (60); Relationships (1570)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Overall risk/impact is health-system and patient-outcome oriented, not malicious or adversarial\",\"explanation\":\"All entities and relationships in this community pertain to medical follow-up processes (surveillance exams, survivorship planning, late-effect screening) and patient support resources, indicating a healthcare coordination and patient-outcome focus rather than any indication of wro